# Frontend Development Rules

## Project Stack
- React 18 with TypeScript
- Vite for build tooling
- TanStack Query (React Query) for data fetching
- Tailwind CSS for styling
- Vitest for testing

## File Size & Organization
- **Maximum 200 lines per file** - If a file exceeds this, refactor into smaller, focused modules
- Split large components into smaller sub-components
- Extract custom hooks when logic becomes complex
- Move utility functions to separate files in `src/utils/`
- Keep related files close together (component, styles, tests, types)

## Testing Requirements
- **Every new component must have tests** - Create `ComponentName.test.tsx` alongside the component
- **Every new feature must have tests** - Include unit tests and integration tests where appropriate
- Test user interactions, not implementation details
- Use Vitest and React Testing Library best practices (query by role, label, text)
- Aim for meaningful test coverage, not just high percentages
- Mock external dependencies (API calls, localStorage, etc.)

## Component Guidelines
- Use functional components with hooks exclusively
- Prefer named exports for components
- Keep components focused on a single responsibility
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Use TypeScript interfaces for props (prefer `interface` over `type` for component props)
- Always type component props explicitly

## React Query Best Practices
- Use `useQuery` for GET requests
- Use `useMutation` for POST/PUT/DELETE requests
- Define query keys as constants in a separate file (e.g queryKeys.ts)
- Implement proper error handling and loading states
- Use `queryClient.invalidateQueries()` after mutations
- Leverage stale time and cache time appropriately

## State Management
- Use Zustand for global state. Only suggest move/use Redux if complexity starts to increase
- Keep state as local as possible - lift only when necessary
- Use React Query for server state, not local state management
- Avoid prop drilling - use context or state management for deeply nested props

## TypeScript Standards
- Enable strict mode
- Avoid `any` type - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Define interfaces for all data structures
- Use type inference where possible, explicit types where clarity is needed
- Create shared types in `src/types/` for reusable interfaces

## Styling with Tailwind
- Use Tailwind utility classes directly in JSX
- Extract repeated patterns into components, not custom CSS
- Use Tailwind's responsive prefixes (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`) for responsive design
- Leverage Tailwind's color palette and spacing scale
- Use `className` prop, never inline styles unless absolutely necessary

## Code Quality
- Follow ESLint rules strictly
- Use Prettier for consistent formatting
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable and function names
- Add comments only when the "why" isn't obvious from the code
- Avoid nested ternaries - use early returns or separate variables
- Keep functions small and focused (max 20-30 lines)

## Performance
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components that re-render frequently
- Implement code splitting with `React.lazy()` for route-based components
- Optimize images and assets
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` judiciously (only when profiling shows benefit)
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders by proper dependency arrays

## Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include proper ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain sufficient color contrast
- Test with screen readers when possible

## Error Handling
- Implement error boundaries for component trees
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately for debugging
- Handle loading and error states in all async operations
- Validate user input on the client side

## Import Organization
- Group imports: React, third-party libraries, local components, utilities, types
- Use absolute imports with path aliases (e.g., `@/components`)
- Avoid circular dependencies
- Remove unused imports

## Git & Version Control
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Create feature branches for new work
- Review your own code before requesting review

